        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-347",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "jose/backends/native.py:HMACKey.__init__ and jose/backends/cryptography_backend.py:CryptographyHMACKey.__init__ guard against algorithm-confusion by rejecting HMAC keys whose byte content contains the markers ['-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----', '-----BEGIN RSA PUBLIC KEY-----', '-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----', 'ssh-rsa']. This denylist is incomplete: it does not include OpenSSH-formatted EC public key markers ('ecdsa-sha2-') or Ed25519 markers ('ssh-ed25519'). An application that holds an EC public key in OpenSSH wire/text format (e.g. for ES256) and calls jose.jwt.decode()/jose.jws.verify() without restricting `algorithms` can be fed an attacker-forged HS256 token where the HMAC 'secret' is simply the known-public EC key text, bypassing signature verification (algorithm-confusion / key-confusion attack), because the only safety net (this string-pattern check) never fires for that key format. This mirrors the pattern of CVE-2024-33663 (python-jose algorithm confusion with OpenSSH ECDSA keys).\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source + graph. Both HMACKey.__init__ (jose/backends/native.py:39-50) and CryptographyHMACKey.__init__ (jose/backends/cryptography_backend.py:555-566) contain the identical denylist `[b\"-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\", b\"-----BEGIN RSA PUBLIC KEY-----\", b\"-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\", b\"ssh-rsa\"]` as the ONLY check preventing an asymmetric key from being accepted as an HMAC secret; it has no entry for OpenSSH-formatted EC keys (`ecdsa-sha2-...`) or Ed25519 (`ssh-ed25519`), so a raw OpenSSH EC/Ed25519 public-key string sails through unblocked. jose/backends/__init__.py:28 confirms CryptographyHMACKey is bound to the public `HMACKey` name whenever `cryptography` is importable (pinned to ==39.0.1 in requirements-dev.txt). I traced the actual exploit path in jws.py: `jws.verify`/`jwt.decode` take an optional `algorithms` param that defaults to None (jwt.py:55, jws.py:48); `_verify_signature` (jws.py:249-264) only enforces an allow-list `if algorithms is not None`; when omitted, `_sig_matches_keys` (jws.py:205-214) calls `jwk.construct(key, alg)` using the attacker-controlled `alg` from the JWS header, and `jwk.construct`/`get_key` (jwk.py:31-79) deterministically map alg=\"HS256\" to `HMACKey(key_data, \"HS256\")` regardless of what the supplied key material actually is. So an app that holds an EC public key in OpenSSH wire format and calls decode()/verify() without restricting `algorithms` is fully exposed to HS256-forged tokens signed with the known public key text as HMAC secret. jose/__init__.py:1 shows this is python-jose 3.3.0, exactly the version named in the public CVE-2024-33663 advisory for this class of OpenSSH-ECDSA algorithm-confusion bug (exploit_search tool returned 'corpus unavailable' in this environment so I could not corroborate via that catalog, but the version/behavior match is independently confirmed from source)."
          },
