        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-347",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "jose.jws.verify() / jose.jwt.decode() do not mandatorily enforce an algorithm allow-list: in jws.py `_verify_signature`, the restriction `algorithms is not None and alg not in algorithms` is only applied when the caller explicitly supplies `algorithms`; jwt.py's decode() forwards its own optional `algorithms` parameter (default None, confirmed by call-site `jws.verify(token, key, algorithms, verify=verify_signature)` with no enforced non-None requirement) straight through. When a caller omits `algorithms`, the attacker-controlled `alg` header value from the token is accepted for ANY registered algorithm family and is used directly to pick the Key class via `jwk.construct(key, alg)` (jwk.py line 79: `key_class(key_data, algorithm)`), with no check binding the algorithm family to the actual type/format of the supplied key material beyond the narrow denylist described in the related finding. This is a missing defense-in-depth control (mandatory algorithm allow-listing) that is a precondition for algorithm-confusion attacks.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived directly from source (not from the analyst's say-so). jose/jws.py:249-264 `_verify_signature(signing_input, header, signature, key=\"\", algorithms=None)` reads the attacker-controlled `alg` from the unverified JWS header (line 250) and only rejects it via `if algorithms is not None and alg not in algorithms:` (line 254) \u2014 i.e. the allow-list check is entirely skipped when the caller omits `algorithms`. It then unconditionally calls `_get_keys(key)` and `_sig_matches_keys(keys, signing_input, signature, alg)`, which at jws.py:208 does `key = jwk.construct(key, alg)` \u2014 constructing the verification Key class purely from the attacker's `alg` value. jose/jwt.py:55 `decode(token, key, algorithms=None, ...)` confirms the default is `None`, and jwt.py:141 `payload = jws.verify(token, key, algorithms, verify=verify_signature)` passes it straight through unmodified/unchecked \u2014 so `jwt.decode(token, key)` called without an explicit `algorithms` list inherits the same unrestricted-`alg` behavior. jwk.py:79 `return key_class(key_data, algorithm)` inside `construct()` confirms the key class is selected solely by the (attacker-supplied) algorithm string via `get_key()`. I also confirmed the \"related\" denylist the analyst alludes to: jose/backends/native.py:39-50 HMACKey.__init__ only blocks keys containing the literal substrings `-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----`, `-----BEGIN RSA PUBLIC KEY-----`, `-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----`, or `ssh-rsa` \u2014 it does not recognize other asymmetric-key encodings (e.g. OpenSSH `ecdsa-sha2-*` keys, raw EC key bytes), so it is a narrow, incomplete safety net, not a substitute for a mandatory algorithm allow-list. Graph query confirms `_verify_signature` (jose/jws.py:249) is `reachable_from_entry=true` with `hop_distance=0`, i.e. it is directly on the library's public verification path, not dead code. This is a genuine, source-confirmed defense-in-depth gap (precondition for alg-confusion / signature-forgery attacks such as RS256\u2194HS256 confusion) that exists whenever a downstream caller invokes `jws.verify()`/`jwt.decode()` without supplying an explicit `algorithms` allow-list. Severity is MEDIUM rather than higher because exploitation requires that caller-side precondition (omitting `algorithms`) plus a key format that slips past the existing HMACKey denylist \u2014 it is not a standalone unauthenticated bypass in this code by itself. Could not corroborate with the exploit_search tool: the exploit corpus is unavailable in this environment (\"not indexed\"), so no CVE/Metasploit-module cross-check was possible \u2014 this verdict rests solely on direct source/graph evidence."
          },
