        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-208",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Declared dependency ecdsa (version constraint '!= 0.15', i.e. any other version is accepted) is used as the pure-Python ECDSA backend (ECDSAECKey.sign/verify in jose/backends/ecdsa_backend.py, both reachable_from_entry=true) for ES256/ES384/ES512 JWS signing and verification. The python-ecdsa package is affected by CVE-2024-23342 (the 'Minerva' timing side-channel attack on ECDSA signing) in all released versions; upstream maintainers have stated they will not fix this because the pure-Python implementation cannot be made constant-time. Any application using this library's EC sign operation with an attacker-measurable timing channel (e.g. a remote signing oracle) is exposed to private-key recovery.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source + graph, not from the analyst's say-so. (1) setup.cfg:35 declares `install_requires = ecdsa != 0.15`, matching the graph's Dependency node exactly \u2014 any ecdsa release except 0.15 is accepted, so whatever vulnerable version a consumer resolves will be installed. (2) jose/backends/__init__.py:17-20 shows ECDSAECKey is used as the EC backend (`ECKey`) by default: it only falls back to the `cryptography`-based implementation if that package (an optional extra in setup.cfg, not a core dependency) is installed, so a bare `pip install python-jose` uses the pure-Python ecdsa backend. (3) jose/constants.py:13-15,56,58 confirms ES256/ES384/ES512 are in ALGORITHMS.EC_DS/EC, and ecdsa_backend.py's ECDSAECKey.__init__ (lines 36-47) maps each to SHA256/384/512 and a NIST curve, confirming these three algs route through this class. (4) jose/jws.py:156-169 (_sign_header_and_claims) and :205-214 (_sig_matches_keys) show the public jws.sign()/jws.verify() API constructs a Key via jwk.construct() and calls key.sign()/key.verify() directly \u2014 i.e. ECDSAECKey.sign (line 98) / .verify (line 103) sit right behind the library's public signing/verification entry points. (5) Graph query confirms both CpgMethod nodes: sign at line 98 and verify at line 103, both reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0 (they are themselves marked as EntryPoint targets, consistent with being public library API). All of the analyst's cited facts check out exactly against the real source; I did not rely on their query output, I re-ran it and read the files myself. The underlying CVE-2024-23342 ('Minerva' non-constant-time scalar multiplication in python-ecdsa, unfixed by upstream for pure-Python builds) is a real, named, well-documented issue, though the orion exploit_search corpus was unavailable in this environment ('not indexed') so I could not cross-check a specific Metasploit module/version boundary \u2014 treat that leg as not independently verified. Exploitation still requires an attacker who can measure fine-grained timing of the EC signing operation (e.g. a remote signing oracle), which is a real but non-trivial precondition, matching the hedge already in the claim."
          },
